EldenNet, Lodestar's finding run (package @LABEL@)
@UNDERLINE@

This package runs the offline finder of Lodestar, EldenNet's own address library, on your own PC (EldenNet's decisions
O100 to O102; proposal 0005, step L3). It reads the game's executable file, and only the file: the game stays closed
and is not started. It writes one small file of numbers, lodestar-found.json, into this folder, and that file is what
comes back to us.

Built from EldenNet's engine branch at commit @COMMIT@ with clang-cl (Release). Never shared: a test build for you
alone, labelled @LABEL@.


What the run is for
-------------------

Four things, each a count or a list of numbers. The fourth is the reason for this run (22 November):

  1. The managers' accessors. Until 21 November EldenNet looked for them with a short byte pattern cut from the
     game's code. That pattern is withdrawn (O102: nothing of the game in EldenNet's files), and a description of our
     own, a "shape", replaces it. On our own test programs the two find exactly the same places. This run counts the
     shape on the real game and says whether it accepts the same places there too (R0c's run accepted 7,081).
  2. The managers' slots. For every slot the accessors load, the run lists where it is and up to eight places in the
     code that use it. EldenNet will check those places every time the game starts.
  3. The character classes and the task groups' table, as R0c found them, in the new report.
  4. For each of the eleven managers EldenNet asks for (the game's main objects: one keeps the world's characters,
     another the time of day, and so on): whether the game's own type information holds a class under the name we
     look for, and how many. The read-only round in the game named eight of the eleven and could not say why the
     other three were missing. These counts tell us: a manager with no such class cannot be found by its class at
     all, and one whose class goes by another name needs its name corrected. Only the counts are written, never a
     name.


How long it takes
-----------------

About 5 minutes: 2 to unzip and find the game's file, 1 or 2 for the run, 1 to send the file.


What is in it
-------------

  find.cmd                The run: drop the game's executable file onto it.
  eldennet-engine.exe     EldenNet's command-line tool, which does the reading (its lodestar command) and checks the
                          report (its lint command).
  gal\                    What to look for: the shape (recipes.toml, with its note in derivations\), the names of the
                          design's own (leads.toml), the build EldenNet knows (builds.toml) and Lodestar's catalogue
                          of ids (ids.toml). Our own words and numbers; no byte, text or name taken from the game.
  README-FIND.txt         This file.
  SHA256SUMS              The SHA-256 of every other file in this package.
  LICENSE.txt             EldenNet's licence (MIT).
  THIRD_PARTY.md          What EldenNet is built with, and its credits.
  THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt The licences of the libraries inside the program.


Before you start
----------------

  - Close the game.
  - Unzip this package into a folder of its own, not inside the game's folder.
  - If you like, check the files: in PowerShell, in this folder, `Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 *` gives each file's
    SHA-256, to compare with SHA256SUMS.


The steps
---------

  1. In Explorer, open the game's install folder and its Game folder, where eldenring.exe is. Do not copy, move or
     change it.
  2. Open this package's folder in a second window.
  3. Drag eldenring.exe onto find.cmd and let go.
  4. A console window opens. After a minute or two it shows one line that starts "lodestar: report written", then
     "lint: the report is clean". Press a key to close it.
  5. Send back, in Lodestar's chat: the file lodestar-found.json from this folder, and the line that starts
     "lodestar: report written".

The line ends with "(complete)" or "(to read first)". Both are results worth sending: "to read first" means the
count was outside what the recipe expects, the shape accepted a place the old pattern did not, or one class object
went with two slots, and that is what we need to see.


What comes back, and what does not
----------------------------------

lodestar-found.json holds whole numbers, true and false, a few fixed words (such as "derived" or "within") and labels
of EldenNet's own (such as "chr_ins"). You can open it in Notepad. It holds no byte of the game, no pattern, no text,
no name read from the game, no code, and no path, user name or machine name of your PC. The tool checks this before it
writes the file, and refuses to write one that breaks it.

The run does not start the game, does not change any file of the game, does not go online, and reads no running
program's memory. The only file it writes is lodestar-found.json, in this folder.


If something goes wrong
-----------------------

The console's last lines give the tool's exit code:

  0   written, complete.
  1   written, to read first: send the file all the same.
  2   find.cmd was started without a file dropped on it, or with more than one.
  3   the file dropped is not the game's executable, or cannot be read. Is the game closed?
  4   a file of gal\ is missing or was changed: unzip the package again.
  5   the report was refused by its own check: a defect of ours. Send the console's lines.
  6   lodestar-found.json could not be written: is this folder writable?

Send the console's lines in any of these cases. They name no path of your PC.


Safe use
--------

@DISCLOSURE@
